1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:02,827
Last Black Friday, Americans spent eleven point eight billion

2
00:00:02,827 --> 00:00:05,605
dollars online. In one day. And a huge part

3
00:00:05,605 --> 00:00:08,384
of that money was spent because of tricks… that

4
00:00:08,384 --> 00:00:09,310
you never noticed.

5
00:00:09,509 --> 00:00:12,717
Eleven point eight billion dollars. A new record, according

6
00:00:12,717 --> 00:00:13,429
to Adobe.

7
00:00:13,630 --> 00:00:16,550
And over the whole Thanksgiving weekend, almost two hundred

8
00:00:16,550 --> 00:00:19,469
and three million people went shopping. Also a record.

9
00:00:19,670 --> 00:00:22,054
Stores know exactly how your brain works. So today,

10
00:00:22,054 --> 00:00:24,439
I'll show you the seven tricks they use… and

11
00:00:24,439 --> 00:00:26,029
how to beat every single one.

12
00:00:26,230 --> 00:00:28,932
But first, a quick story. Why is it even

13
00:00:28,932 --> 00:00:31,635
called Black Friday? Many people think it's because stores

14
00:00:31,635 --> 00:00:34,338
finally go from losing money, in the red, to

15
00:00:34,338 --> 00:00:35,840
making money, in the black.

16
00:00:36,039 --> 00:00:38,617
That's a nice story, but the name actually started

17
00:00:38,617 --> 00:00:41,195
with the police in Philadelphia in the nineteen fifties

18
00:00:41,195 --> 00:00:43,772
and sixties. They called it Black Friday because of

19
00:00:43,772 --> 00:00:46,350
the traffic jams and chaos the day after Thanksgiving.

20
00:00:46,549 --> 00:00:49,299
Stores tried to rebrand it as Big Friday. It

21
00:00:49,299 --> 00:00:52,049
didn't work. And today, it's the biggest shopping event

22
00:00:52,049 --> 00:00:54,799
of the year. Okay. Let's get into the tricks.

23
00:00:55,299 --> 00:00:56,020
Trick number one.

24
00:00:56,520 --> 00:00:59,570
You see a TV: was eight hundred, now four

25
00:00:59,570 --> 00:01:02,619
ninety-nine. Wow. Except… was it ever really eight hundred?

26
00:01:02,820 --> 00:01:05,246
That big crossed-out number is called an anchor. Your

27
00:01:05,246 --> 00:01:07,672
brain compares the new price to it, so anything

28
00:01:07,672 --> 00:01:09,019
below feels like a win.

29
00:01:09,219 --> 00:01:12,257
Psychologists Amos Tversky and Daniel Kahneman showed how powerful

30
00:01:12,257 --> 00:01:15,294
anchors are back in the nineteen seventies. Even random

31
00:01:15,294 --> 00:01:18,332
numbers can change how much people guess something is

32
00:01:18,332 --> 00:01:18,669
worth.

33
00:01:18,870 --> 00:01:21,516
The British consumer group Which? tracked Black Friday deals

34
00:01:21,516 --> 00:01:24,162
for months before and after the sale. In their

35
00:01:24,162 --> 00:01:26,808
twenty twenty-three study, ninety-two percent were the same price

36
00:01:26,808 --> 00:01:29,160
or cheaper at other times of the year.

37
00:01:29,359 --> 00:01:32,190
And in another Which? study, only about two percent

38
00:01:32,190 --> 00:01:35,021
of discounted products were actually at their cheapest price

39
00:01:35,021 --> 00:01:36,280
on Black Friday itself.

40
00:01:36,480 --> 00:01:39,287
How to beat it: ignore the was price completely.

41
00:01:39,287 --> 00:01:42,095
Check the price history with free tools like Keepa

42
00:01:42,095 --> 00:01:42,719
or CamelCamelCamel.

43
00:01:43,219 --> 00:01:44,079
Trick number two.

44
00:01:44,579 --> 00:01:47,365
Why do prices end in ninety-nine? Because we read

45
00:01:47,365 --> 00:01:50,150
from left to right. Nine ninety-nine feels like nine.

46
00:01:50,150 --> 00:01:50,769
Not ten.

47
00:01:50,969 --> 00:01:53,640
It's called the left-digit effect. The first digit grabs

48
00:01:53,640 --> 00:01:56,311
most of your attention. That's why a car at

49
00:01:56,311 --> 00:01:58,982
nineteen thousand nine hundred feels much cheaper than twenty

50
00:01:58,982 --> 00:02:01,060
thousand, even though the difference is tiny.

51
00:02:01,260 --> 00:02:04,605
Researchers from MIT and the University of Chicago tested

52
00:02:04,605 --> 00:02:07,950
the same item at thirty-four, thirty-nine and forty-four dollars.

53
00:02:07,950 --> 00:02:11,294
The thirty-nine dollar version sold the most. More than

54
00:02:11,294 --> 00:02:12,409
the cheaper one.

55
00:02:12,610 --> 00:02:15,465
How to beat it: round every price up in

56
00:02:15,465 --> 00:02:18,319
your head. Ninety-nine ninety-nine is one hundred dollars. Period.

57
00:02:18,819 --> 00:02:19,719
Trick number three.

58
00:02:20,219 --> 00:02:24,207
Behavioral economist Dan Ariely showed students three subscriptions. Online

59
00:02:24,207 --> 00:02:28,194
for fifty-nine dollars. Print for one twenty-five. And print

60
00:02:28,194 --> 00:02:30,409
plus online… also one twenty-five.

61
00:02:30,610 --> 00:02:34,178
With that useless print-only option, eighty-four percent picked the

62
00:02:34,178 --> 00:02:37,349
expensive combo. Without it, only thirty-two percent did.

63
00:02:37,550 --> 00:02:40,091
On Black Friday you'll see it everywhere: small, medium

64
00:02:40,091 --> 00:02:42,632
and large, where the medium only exists to make

65
00:02:42,632 --> 00:02:45,173
the large look smart. How to beat it: ask

66
00:02:45,173 --> 00:02:47,715
which option you would choose if the middle one

67
00:02:47,715 --> 00:02:48,280
didn't exist.

68
00:02:48,479 --> 00:02:50,893
You'll see the same thing with bundles, popcorn sizes

69
00:02:50,893 --> 00:02:53,306
and phone storage. The option right below the most

70
00:02:53,306 --> 00:02:55,719
expensive one is there to make the most expensive

71
00:02:55,719 --> 00:02:57,060
one feel like a deal.

72
00:02:57,560 --> 00:02:58,469
Trick number four.

73
00:02:58,969 --> 00:03:01,605
Deal ends in fifty-nine minutes. Only two left in

74
00:03:01,605 --> 00:03:04,240
stock. Twelve people are looking at this right now.

75
00:03:04,439 --> 00:03:07,207
This is scarcity. When something feels rare or about

76
00:03:07,207 --> 00:03:09,975
to disappear, we stop thinking and start grabbing. And

77
00:03:09,975 --> 00:03:12,742
many of these timers simply reset when they hit

78
00:03:12,742 --> 00:03:13,050
zero.

79
00:03:13,250 --> 00:03:16,090
Back in nineteen seventy-five, researchers gave people a jar

80
00:03:16,090 --> 00:03:18,931
with ten cookies and a jar with just two.

81
00:03:18,931 --> 00:03:21,771
The cookies from the almost empty jar were rated

82
00:03:21,771 --> 00:03:23,349
as more desirable. Same cookies.

83
00:03:23,550 --> 00:03:26,189
How to beat it: if a deal disappears, another

84
00:03:26,189 --> 00:03:28,829
one will come. Black Friday deals keep coming back

85
00:03:28,829 --> 00:03:31,469
for weeks. Use the twenty-four hour rule for anything

86
00:03:31,469 --> 00:03:32,349
that isn't essential.

87
00:03:32,849 --> 00:03:33,860
Trick number five.

88
00:03:34,360 --> 00:03:37,107
Four easy payments of twenty-five dollars sounds way cheaper

89
00:03:37,107 --> 00:03:39,854
than one hundred dollars. But it's the same hundred

90
00:03:39,854 --> 00:03:40,159
dollars.

91
00:03:40,360 --> 00:03:42,995
And people love it. On Cyber Monday twenty twenty-five,

92
00:03:42,995 --> 00:03:45,630
buy now pay later drove over one billion dollars

93
00:03:45,630 --> 00:03:47,680
in online spending in a single day.

94
00:03:47,879 --> 00:03:50,163
The problem: when you stack several of these plans,

95
00:03:50,163 --> 00:03:52,447
it's easy to lose track. Miss a payment, and

96
00:03:52,447 --> 00:03:54,731
you can get hit with late fees. How to

97
00:03:54,731 --> 00:03:57,014
beat it: only use it if you could pay

98
00:03:57,014 --> 00:03:58,029
the full price today.

99
00:03:58,229 --> 00:04:00,693
And there's a psychological effect too: spreading out a

100
00:04:00,693 --> 00:04:03,157
payment makes each piece feel smaller, so it's easier

101
00:04:03,157 --> 00:04:04,800
to add more to the cart.

102
00:04:05,300 --> 00:04:06,349
Trick number six.

103
00:04:06,849 --> 00:04:09,560
Your cart is thirty dollars. Free shipping starts at

104
00:04:09,560 --> 00:04:12,270
thirty-five. So you add a twelve dollar item you

105
00:04:12,270 --> 00:04:14,679
didn't need… to save six dollars on shipping.

106
00:04:14,879 --> 00:04:17,305
You just spent twelve to save six. How to

107
00:04:17,305 --> 00:04:19,731
beat it: if you don't need it, pay the

108
00:04:19,731 --> 00:04:20,540
shipping. It's cheaper.

109
00:04:20,740 --> 00:04:23,242
Retailers set the free shipping line just above the

110
00:04:23,242 --> 00:04:25,744
average order on purpose. That little gap is designed

111
00:04:25,744 --> 00:04:27,689
to make you add one more thing.

112
00:04:28,189 --> 00:04:29,180
And trick number seven.

113
00:04:29,680 --> 00:04:32,043
That crazy cheap TV in the ad? Stores often

114
00:04:32,043 --> 00:04:34,406
have only a few of them. Some even sell

115
00:04:34,406 --> 00:04:36,770
them at a loss. It's called a loss leader.

116
00:04:36,970 --> 00:04:39,634
Because once you're in the store or on the

117
00:04:39,634 --> 00:04:42,299
website, you'll buy the cables, the warranty, the accessories…

118
00:04:42,299 --> 00:04:43,780
where the profit really is.

119
00:04:43,980 --> 00:04:45,920
How to beat it: write a list before you

120
00:04:45,920 --> 00:04:47,430
go… and buy only what's on it.

121
00:04:47,930 --> 00:04:51,139
Bonus trick: the emails. Early access. VIP deals. Only

122
00:04:51,139 --> 00:04:54,348
for you. These messages are designed to make you

123
00:04:54,348 --> 00:04:57,557
feel special… and to start the shopping before you've

124
00:04:57,557 --> 00:04:59,340
even decided you need anything.

125
00:04:59,839 --> 00:05:02,218
How to beat it: unsubscribe from the stores you

126
00:05:02,218 --> 00:05:04,597
don't need, or at least turn off app notifications

127
00:05:04,597 --> 00:05:05,389
for the week.

128
00:05:05,589 --> 00:05:08,221
And remember the most important number: not how much

129
00:05:08,221 --> 00:05:10,852
you saved… but how much you spent. A fifty

130
00:05:10,852 --> 00:05:13,483
percent discount on something you didn't need is not

131
00:05:13,483 --> 00:05:14,360
saving. It's spending.

132
00:05:14,560 --> 00:05:17,437
So here's your Black Friday cheat sheet. Ignore the

133
00:05:17,437 --> 00:05:20,314
was price. Round up. Skip the decoy. Wait twenty-four

134
00:05:20,314 --> 00:05:23,192
hours. Only pay later if you could pay now.

135
00:05:23,192 --> 00:05:25,750
Pay for shipping. And stick to your list.

136
00:05:25,949 --> 00:05:28,556
Which trick have you fallen for? Be honest, tell

137
00:05:28,556 --> 00:05:31,163
me in the comments. And subscribe, because next time

138
00:05:31,163 --> 00:05:33,770
I'll show you how companies trick you every single

139
00:05:33,770 --> 00:05:34,059
month.
